WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.756
Authorization architecture defines where decisions are made and where they are enforced.

2
00:00:05.756 --> 00:00:10.241
A policy decision point, at a high level, evaluates policy and context.

3
00:00:10.241 --> 00:00:15.548
A policy enforcement point applies the result by allowing or denying the operation.

4
00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:27.283
In practice, these may be centralized services, application-local checks, middleware, guard clauses, framework annotations, database controls, or combinations of those patterns.

5
00:00:27.283 --> 00:00:35.655
Centralized authorization can improve consistency and governance, but it must be integrated carefully with application context.

6
00:00:35.655 --> 00:00:43.429
Application-local checks can be close to the code and easier to understand, but they may become inconsistent across teams.

7
00:00:43.429 --> 00:00:49.633
Reusable authorization middleware and guard clauses can help make safe behavior easier to apply.

8
00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:57.258
The architecture should make correct checks routine rather than relying on every developer to remember every edge case.

9
00:00:57.258 --> 00:01:05.629
Data filtering and database-layer controls at a high level can support authorization by limiting which records queries can return.

10
00:01:05.629 --> 00:01:10.488
However, data filtering should not be confused with complete policy design.

11
00:01:10.488 --> 00:01:19.009
The system still needs to know which actions are allowed, how tenant boundaries are enforced, and how administrative exceptions work.

12
00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:28.054
Caching authorization decisions can improve performance, but stale permissions can create risk if changes are not reflected quickly enough.

13
00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:31.642
Fail-closed behavior and error handling are important.

14
00:01:31.642 --> 00:01:38.668
If the authorization system cannot evaluate a decision, the safer default is usually to deny rather than allow.

15
00:01:38.668 --> 00:01:45.022
Error messages should not reveal sensitive resources or policy internals to unauthorized callers.

16
00:01:45.022 --> 00:01:53.170
A strong architecture makes safe behavior consistent, observable, and hard to bypass accidentally as the application evolves.
